# Copy to .env (gitignored) — `make run` sources it before starting the
# server. Anything here can also be provided via the environment; note that
# for `make run`, values set in .env take precedence over inherited env vars.

# Required: Anthropic API key (the primary upstream).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api03-...

# Optional: enables OpenRouter failover on /v1/messages and the
# /v1/chat/completions face. Without it the server runs primary-only.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...

# Optional: conversation-store DSN. `make run` defaults to the local
# rafiki-test-db container (see README); set explicitly to pin a different
# database, or leave the server capture-less by passing an empty --db.
#RAFIKI_DB=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/rafiki_live?sslmode=disable

# Optional: OTLP tracing endpoint (standard OTEL_* env vars honored;
# unset = tracing disabled, zero overhead).
#OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317

# Optional, read ONLY by `make claude` (never exported to the server):
# retarget the client at a non-default rafiki instance. RAFIKI_URL/RAFIKI_TOKEN
# are client-side (what this process presents to a proxy) and are also what
# `rafikid` reads to point its own children at an EXTERNAL rafiki instead of
# the embedded face — see RAFIKI_SERVE_TOKEN below for the opposite direction.
#RAFIKI_URL=http://localhost:8035
#RAFIKI_TOKEN=dev

# Optional, read ONLY by the daemon serving its own proxy face: one
# ADDITIONAL token the face ACCEPTS, for humans and tools that are not
# spawned children (`rafiki claude`, an editor plugin, curl) and so cannot
# know the per-boot child secret. `make run` sets this to `dev` so
# `make claude`'s default RAFIKI_TOKEN works out of the box. Server-side,
# the opposite direction from RAFIKI_TOKEN above.
#RAFIKI_SERVE_TOKEN=dev


#############################################################################
# rafiki daemon / CLI — the sections below configure rafikid, rafiki and attach.
#############################################################################

# Copy to .env (gitignored, along with .env-*). Nothing in this repo sources
# it for you — there is no `make run`, and the Makefile never reads .env — so
# load it yourself before starting the daemon:
#
#     set -a && . ./.env && set +a && ./bin/rafikid
#
# What matters is that these reach the DAEMON's environment, not your shell's.
# `rafikid agent` (the "agent" kind) is the daemon re-exec'ing itself, so an
# agent child inherits the daemon's env. `rafiki create --forward-env` (on by
# default) forwards the CALLER's env to the child on top of that, but it does
# not help the flags resolved from the daemon's own environment — notably
# RAFIKI_DB. Export it in the shell you run `rafiki service install` from and
# it is written to ~/.config/rafiki/service.env (0600) — never into the
# launchd plist or systemd unit, which are world-readable and would expose the
# DSN's password.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- credentials

# Required for anthropic/* models (native Anthropic SDK path).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api03-...

# Required for every non-anthropic model: model ids are single
# provider-qualified knobs, and anything that isn't anthropic/* is routed via
# OpenRouter (e.g. deepseek/deepseek-chat). There is no --provider flag.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ persistence

# REQUIRED for per-turn cost accounting: postgres DSN for agent conversation
# persistence. Unset means in-memory — conversations vanish when the child
# exits, `rafiki resume` has nothing to reattach to, and NO per-turn token/cost
# data is recorded anywhere. That cost data is the whole point of the dogfood
# loop this daemon exists to feed, so this is not an optional nicety: set it
# in the daemon's service environment before daily use begins.
#
# The tell is the sessionId that `rafiki create` prints back: "mem-..." is
# in-memory, a UUIDv7 is DB-backed. If you set this and still get "mem-...",
# it did not reach the daemon.
#
# rafiki's conversations live in its own `conversations` schema, so pointing
# this at a rafiki database is the intended setup, not a hack.
#RAFIKI_DB=postgres://postgres@localhost:5432/rafiki?sslmode=disable

# ------------------------------------------------------------- agent config
#
# THESE MUST BE SET IN THE DAEMON'S ENVIRONMENT, not your shell.
#
# rafiki reads its own agent-runtime configuration from its own directories
# by default. These three point it elsewhere instead — e.g. at a Claude Code
# profile or a shared plugin skills cache.
#
# `rafiki create --forward-env` (on by default) forwards the CALLER's env to
# the child, but collectCallerEnv (cmd/rafiki/cmd_create.go) strips EVERY
# RAFIKI_* key (and, belt-and-suspenders, FUNDI_* and PI_CONTROLLER_*, the
# two prior spellings) before building that forwarded set — deliberately, so a
# stale shell export can't override what the daemon injects per child (the
# child's socket path and child id are injected the same way and must not be
# spoofable by caller env). Exporting
# RAFIKI_INSTRUCTIONS / RAFIKI_SKILLS_DIRS / RAFIKI_MCP_CONFIG in your own shell
# and running `rafiki create` therefore does nothing at all, silently — same
# rule as RAFIKI_DB above. Set them in the launchd/systemd unit's
# environment, or export them in the shell you start `rafikid` from.
#
# Note the asymmetry: XDG_CONFIG_HOME is NOT stripped, so a caller's value
# DOES reach the child via --forward-env, and every path below is resolved as
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rafiki/... (pkg/paths/paths.go's ConfigDir), so it
# relocates instructions.md, skills/, presets.json, and mcp.json all at once.
# That's arguably useful — a supported way to point one invocation at a whole
# separate config tree — but it sits right next to three variables that
# behave the opposite way, so don't assume the same rule applies to all four.
#
# There's no sessionId-style tell for whether these three reached the daemon
# — and a naive check gives the WRONG answer. A merely-absent path is treated
# as "nothing there, nothing to say" and logs silently (os.IsNotExist is
# explicitly not-an-error in loadInstructionFile): pointing RAFIKI_INSTRUCTIONS
# at a file that doesn't exist proves nothing either way.
#
# The concrete check that actually works: point it at a path *underneath* an
# existing regular file, e.g. RAFIKI_INSTRUCTIONS=/etc/hosts/nope, so the stat
# fails with ENOTDIR instead of "not exist". Restart rafikid, spawn an agent
# child, let it exit, then `rafiki logs --err <child>` (matching the daemon's
# XDG_STATE_HOME if you've overridden it, since the CLI reads the on-disk dump
# itself). Verified 2026-07-29: that setup logs
#   level=WARN msg="agent: failed to stat instruction file" path=/etc/hosts/nope error="stat /etc/hosts/nope: not a directory"
# while the same steps with a plain missing path log nothing at all. If you
# don't see the warning, the variable didn't reach the daemon; if you do, it
# did — but only this ENOTDIR-shaped path can tell you that.

# User-global instruction file (layered like CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md context, but
# from rafiki's own path). Default: ~/.config/rafiki/instructions.md
#RAFIKI_INSTRUCTIONS=~/.claude-personal/CLAUDE.md

# Skill directories, OS path-list separated (":" on unix), lowest precedence
# first. <cwd>/.claude/skills and <cwd>/.rafiki/skills are still layered on
# top of these per-project, and --skills-dir flags win over all of it.
# Default: ~/.config/rafiki/skills
#RAFIKI_SKILLS_DIRS=~/.claude-personal/skills:~/.claude-personal/plugins/cache/superpowers-marketplace/superpowers/6.2.0/skills

# Global .mcp.json, used when <cwd>/.mcp.json is absent (the per-cwd file,
# and an explicit --mcp-config, both still win). Default: ~/.config/rafiki/mcp.json
#RAFIKI_MCP_CONFIG=~/.config/rafiki/mcp.json

# Global lsp.json, used when <cwd>/.lsp.json is absent (the per-cwd file,
# and an explicit --lsp-config, both still win). Default: ~/.config/rafiki/lsp.json
# When no lsp.json exists anywhere, rafikid auto-detects well-known LSP servers
# on PATH (gopls, rust-analyzer, typescript-language-server, pyright,
# lua-language-server). Set RAFIKI_LSP_DISABLE=1 to suppress this.
# Example lsp.json:
#   { "servers": { "go": { "command": "gopls", "args": [], "extensions": [".go"] } } }
#RAFIKI_LSP_CONFIG=~/.config/rafiki/lsp.json
#RAFIKI_LSP_DISABLE=1

# ----------------------------------------------------------- optional knobs

# Controller socket path. Default is the XDG runtime path
# (~/.local/state/rafiki/controller.sock). Must be an ABSOLUTE path — the value
# is used verbatim, with no tilde expansion. Note pi-controller is a SEPARATE
# daemon on ~/.pi/run/controller.sock — both can run at once, so an override
# pointed at the wrong one connects to the wrong daemon instead of erroring.
#RAFIKI_SOCKET=/Users/you/.local/state/rafiki/controller.sock

# Spawn defaults, applied before explicit flags (lowest priority).
# The model must be provider-qualified; a bare or unset model is an error.
# The preset name is looked up in ~/.config/rafiki/presets.json.
#RAFIKI_DEFAULT_MODEL=anthropic/sonnet-latest
#RAFIKI_DEFAULT_PRESET=your-preset-name
#RAFIKI_DEFAULT_LABELS=owner=you,env=dev

# How long an exited child's record is retained, in hours (float accepted).
# Defaults to 7 days.
#RAFIKI_GRACE_HOURS=24

# Path to the pi binary used for `--kind pi` children (rafiki's knob, not pi's).
#RAFIKI_PI_BINARY=/usr/local/bin/pi

# Non-empty stops `rafiki create` auto-installing/upgrading the bundled
# rafiki-helpers pi extension.
#RAFIKI_NO_AUTO_INSTALL_HELPERS=1

# Debug: when "1", records every raw LLM API request+response to the
# conversations.raw_http_request hypertable. Off by default; only enable
# temporarily for debugging specific sessions. Per-child, use
# `rafiki create --record-requests` to opt in individual sessions.
#RAFIKI_RECORD_REQUESTS=1

# Route the fundi bash tool through rtk for command-aware output compression.
# auto (default) uses rtk when installed, on requires it, off disables it.
# Override per-agent with `rafikid fundi --bash-rtk`.
#RAFIKI_BASH_RTK=auto

# Enable the fundi webfetch and websearch tools. Default off: fundi runs
# unattended and may have no egress. Set to "1" to enable.
#RAFIKI_TOOLS_WEB=1

# Use the Brave Search API for websearch instead of scraping DuckDuckGo.
# Optional: with no key, websearch scrapes DuckDuckGo Lite, which works with
# no setup but can break on a markup change or be served a bot-detection
# page — and to a model an unparseable page looks exactly like "the web has
# nothing on this topic". Brave has a versioned contract and a real error
# channel, so failures are reportable. The free tier allows about one
# request per second, which websearch paces for you.
# Key from https://api.search.brave.com/
#RAFIKI_BRAVE_API_KEY=

# Absolute ceiling on how deep the agent spawn tree may go, counted from a
# top-level child (which is depth 0). Enforced by the daemon against stored
# lineage, independently of what any agent granted its child. Default 3.
# RAFIKI_MAX_DEPTH=3

# Default executor socket for `rafiki create --executor-socket`. Unset means
# children run their filesystem and shell tools in-process.
# RAFIKI_EXECUTOR_SOCKET=

# Enables the executor endpoint. Any non-empty value turns it on; unset means
# executors are refused entirely.
#
# It is a flag, not an address: executors are reached at the PATH
# /executor/connect on the control listener below, upgraded out of HTTP/1.1, so
# ONE port and ONE certificate serve the control plane and the executor link.
# Requires RAFIKI_CONTROL_LISTEN (plus its cert/key).
#
# Kept as an explicit opt-in so that turning on a TCP control plane does not
# silently also open executor enrollment.
# RAFIKI_EXECUTORS_ENABLED=1

# ------------------------------------------------------- control plane (TCP/TLS)
#
# Optional: enables a TCP+TLS control listener for remote clients (k8s deploy,
# laptop dial). Unset = UDS only (local launchd installs unchanged).
#
# TLS is MANDATORY on TCP — the daemon refuses to start without all three:
# RAFIKI_CONTROL_LISTEN, _TLS_CERT, _TLS_KEY, and RAFIKI_CONTROL_TOKEN.
# The cert files are re-read per handshake, so cert-manager rotation needs no
# pod restart.
#
# Clients present the token as the first frame (ctrl_auth) over TLS. Token
# resolution on the client side: RAFIKI_CONTROL_TOKEN env, else
# ~/.config/rafiki/control.token (0600). The daemon reads ONLY the env var.
#
#RAFIKI_CONTROL_LISTEN=tcp:8036
#RAFIKI_CONTROL_TOKEN=…
#RAFIKI_CONTROL_TLS_CERT=/etc/ssl/certs/rafiki.graveland.dev.pem
#RAFIKI_CONTROL_TLS_KEY=/etc/ssl/private/rafiki.graveland.dev.pem
#RAFIKI_CONTROL_URL=tls://rafiki.graveland.dev:443

# Read by the TypeScript side (rafiki-attach / rafiki-helpers), not by Go.
#RAFIKI_ATTACH_TAIL=2000
#RAFIKI_ATTACH_DEBUG=1
#RAFIKI_KILL_ON_EXIT=1

# Event buffer: coalescing of externally-injected agent events (subagent
# settles, budget warnings, executor loss). A flush is deferred while the
# child is mid-turn and released on its idle transition.
RAFIKI_EVENTBUF_DEBOUNCE_MS=5000
RAFIKI_EVENTBUF_MAX_WAIT_MS=60000
RAFIKI_EVENTBUF_MAX_FRAGMENTS=30
RAFIKI_EVENTBUF_MAX_BYTES_PER_FRAGMENT=8192

# Do NOT set RAFIKI_CHILD_ID here: the daemon sets it per child, and `rafikid
# agent` uses it as the default --ref, which is how a resumed child reattaches
# its stored conversation. Pinning it globally would collide every child onto
# one conversation.

# The pre-rename spellings (FUNDI_*, and before that PI_CONTROLLER_* / PIC_*)
# are retired: nothing falls back to them any more. See pkg/paths for the
# full inventory; it is the single source of truth for what rafiki reads from
# the environment.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── service.env ──
#
# THIS file is for interactive use — you source it yourself. The DAEMON reads a
# different one: ~/.config/rafiki/service.env (override with RAFIKI_ENV_FILE),
# loaded at startup, real environment winning over it. If you export
# RAFIKI_ENV_FILE in the shell you run `rafiki service install` from, it gets
# baked into the unit too (it's a path, not a secret) — otherwise the
# installer would write secrets to your custom path while the daemon kept
# reading the default one and found nothing there.
#
# Two things belong there rather than in the launchd plist or systemd unit:
#
#   1. Credentials. Unit files are world-readable (0644), so `rafiki service
#      install` deliberately refuses to bake ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or
#      OPENROUTER_API_KEY into one. But agent children resolve their key from
#      the daemon's environment first (daemon env < forwarded env < explicit
#      --api-key), so a child spawned with no caller has only that to fall back
#      on. service.env is how the daemon holds one safely — chmod 600 it.
#
#      `rafiki service install` writes this file for you: RAFIKI_DB,
#      RAFIKI_TOKEN, RAFIKI_SERVE_TOKEN, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and
#      OPENROUTER_API_KEY go here, everything else goes in the unit. It only
#      ever APPENDS keys the file does not already define, so hand-edits and
#      a multi-line ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS survive a reinstall untouched. If
#      it finds the file already exists looser than 0600 AND it has a new
#      credential to append, it tightens the file to 0600 first and says so;
#      if it has nothing new to add, it only warns, leaving the file alone.
#
#   2. Anything containing a newline. ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS accepts a literal
#      newline and NOTHING else as its separator (comma, semicolon and a \n
#      escape each silently collapse into one malformed header), and systemd's
#      Environment= is line-based, so a multi-header value cannot be expressed
#      in a unit at all. `rafiki service install` does not capture
#      ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS from anywhere — it is in neither list above —
#      so there is nothing for it to skip or report; add it to service.env by
#      hand, as shown below.
#
# Both spellings produce a real newline here:
#   ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS="X-A: 1\nX-B: 2"
#   ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS="X-A: 1
#   X-B: 2"
